Nightshade Intolerance
Integrative & Functional medicine
GI distress following tomato/potato/eggplant consumption, Widespread migrating joint aching, Exacerbation of chronic inflammatory conditions, Resolution on nightshade-free diet

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-027
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of intractable abdominal pain, severe vomiting, and widespread agonizing joint pain requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute bowel obstruction or acute cholecystitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden, severe abdominal cramping, repeated emesis, and incapacitating joint stiffness following a meal. Urgent on-site physical and abdominal assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute surgical abdomen, acute biliary colic, or severe systemic infection, preventing profound metabolic derangement or bowel ischemia prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is in complete agony, I can't stop throwing up, and every single joint in my body is throbbing."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a rapid onset of severe, sudden, and distinct intense gastrointestinal distress beginning 4 hours after consuming a large meal heavily featuring tomatoes, eggplant, and bell peppers. The patient describes intense severe abdominal cramping, profound severe bloating, and three episodes of non-bilious vomiting. Concurrently, the patient complains of widespread vague migrating joint aching and distinct profound severe muscle stiffness that has abruptly worsened over the past 2 hours, making ambulation nearly impossible. This acute episode exacerbates an underlying chronic inflammatory condition. The patient also reports severe brain fog, distinct severe unprovoked tension headaches, and profound generalized fatigue unalleviated by rest. The spouse notes a distinct new-onset severe skin rash on the patient's torso.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively nauseated, frequently clutching the abdomen.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute dehydration due to intractable vomiting; spouse is highly anxious and unable to manage the pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 108 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, diaphoretic, in obvious physical distress, and avoids any unnecessary movement due to joint pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals profound, generalized distension. The abdomen is tympanitic to percussion and diffusely tender to palpation, but entirely lacks true rigidity, localized right upper quadrant tenderness (negative Murphy's sign), or rebound peritonism. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates widespread, profound severe muscle stiffness and diffuse migrating joint aching without localized, hot, erythematous monoarticular effusion. Skin exam reveals a diffuse, blanching, macular erythematous rash across the torso. Neurological exam reveals an intact sensorium but marked cognitive sluggishness. The clinical picture perfectly aligns with the tetrad of GI distress following nightshade consumption, widespread migrating joint aching, and exacerbation of chronic inflammatory conditions. Confirmation relies on the distinct total resolution of symptoms strictly following the complete removal of dietary nightshades.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen counters show remains of a heavily tomato- and eggplant-based meal.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter NSAIDs for joint pain (currently ineffective); no current immunosuppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Nightshade Intolerance (Severe Dietary Flare) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis / Biliary Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized right upper quadrant tenderness, negative Murphy's sign, and non-bilious emesis.

Acute Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of hyperactive (not silent or high-pitched tinkling) bowel sounds, non-bilious vomiting, and continued passage of flatus.

Acute Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent, simultaneous presentation of severe widespread polyarthralgia and an acute skin rash, which points strongly to a systemic inflammatory/dietary trigger rather than a localized viral gut infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal cramping, repeated vomiting, and widespread systemic inflammation. Telephone triage correctly identified the emergent need to evaluate for an acute surgical abdomen, acute biliary disease, or impending severe dehydration. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to auscultate bowel sounds, perform deep abdominal palpation to check for peritonism, and evaluate hydration status. The physical exclusion of a rigid abdomen, rebound tenderness, and bilious emesis confirmed the absence of an immediate surgical emergency. However, the severity of the painful abdominal spasms, the inability to retain oral fluids, and the debilitating joint stiffness dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of severe dehydration, uncontrolled pain, and extreme physical distress. This urgent evaluation unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered sublingual Ondansetron 4mg stat to break the acute vomiting cycle. Advised strict bowel rest (clear fluids only) for 12 hours. Prescribed a short course of Paracetamol 1g QID PRN for joint analgesia (avoiding NSAIDs to protect the gut mucosa). Recommend routine GP follow-up for inflammatory marker testing and dietary support.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute mucosal and systemic inflammation, initiate specific dietary and hydration protocols. Action: Consume 500ml of an oral rehydration solution (electrolyte fluid) slowly in small sips over the next 3 hours. Consequence: Targeted electrolyte replacement directly restores intravascular volume without overwhelming the nauseated stomach, explicitly rewarding the patient with the cessation of tachycardia and dizziness. Action: Implement a strict 14-day absolute elimination of all nightshades (tomatoes, potatoes, eggplants, bell peppers, chili flakes). Consequence: Removing the specific alkaloid triggers physically halts the immune cross-reactivity in the gut and joints, directly rewarding the patient with a clearance of the severe joint pain and abdominal bloating.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours to assess hydration and joint status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing sudden, unbearable "knife-like" abdominal pain, vomiting blood or material that looks like coffee grounds, passing large amounts of bright red blood or black tarry stools, or developing a high fever with a rigid, board-like stomach.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute vomiting will stop within 30 minutes with the Ondansetron. The abdominal cramping and joint stiffness will take 48-72 hours to fully clear as the inflammatory alkaloids are metabolized.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if your stomach becomes hard as a rock, if you start vomiting blood, or if the pain becomes a sudden, sharp stabbing sensation that does not let up.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is having a severe inflammatory reaction to the specific chemical compounds (alkaloids) found in the nightshade family. This triggered a massive flush of your digestive tract and sent inflammatory signals to your joints and skin.
Actionable Steps: Take the Ondansetron to stop the vomiting. Sip the electrolyte fluid very slowly tonight. For the next two weeks, you must read every label and eat absolutely zero nightshades to let your system completely reset.

